// Services: what `spawn`, a send, a reply and `shutdown` lower to. A service is a cown whose value is its state, with its
// mailbox's gate after the cown's words (serviceGateOffset), and each message is a behaviour on that one cown, so the
// cown's queue is the mailbox and two messages of one service never run at once, preempted or not: a behaviour holds its
// cown until its thunk releases it. The release hands the next message to the green thread that ran the last, which runs
// up to a turn of them (runTurn), so an idle service holds no green thread and a busy one one. The gate counts the
// messages a send has queued and the service has not started, as maxon's does, and holds the service's flags above them.
// A mailbox has no capacity: a send fails only once the service is shut down.
// A reply is a cell shaped like a green thread that is awaited (greenAwaited), which never runs: the message's behaviour
// gives it its value and error as a coroutine's finish would, so an await of it is an await of a coroutine.
//
// Backpressure is Pony's (ponyc's actor.c): the runtime slows the senders of a service that falls behind, so overload
// moves upstream to where it can stop, and no program chooses a number.
//
// - A service slows its senders while it is behind: when a send finds a turn's worth (turnBehaviours) of its messages
//   queued, counting its own; when a turn of its messages ends at turnBehaviours with messages still queued (ponyc's
//   batch_limit_reached), until a turn empties its queue; and while it is muted. The count and both flags are in the gate
//   that every send compare-exchanges already, below and above the count, so one comparison of the word the send took its
//   place with against turnBehaviours decides it: a send to a service that is not behind costs what a send cost before.
//   The length rule bounds the queue in messages, whatever the speeds; the turn rule slows senders sooner where the
//   service runs whole turns without catching up.
// - A slowed sender waits until the receiver catches up, which is when a turn empties its queue, not when it falls back
//   under a turn's worth: a sender that alone outruns a receiver is slowed once per turn's worth it hands on, never on
//   every message.
// - A service that sends to one that is behind is listed on it and muted at the end of a handler: the behaviour's release
//   is kept, so the service holds its cown and runs no next message, nothing waits inside a handler, and no green thread
//   is held, only the behaviour's record (behaviourRelease). A task, a green thread that runs no message (main, a
//   coroutine, a `when` that is no message of a service), parks at the send instead.
// - A listed service that is behind itself, or has senders waiting on it, drains on until it holds no message, and is
//   muted then (drainsOn): it is not stopped while it is overloaded (ponyc mutes a sender only if it is not overloaded
//   itself), but its own senders are slowed, so what it passes on is what it holds, and it cannot pass on a refill.
// - A service that catches up unmutes its senders in the order they were muted, a turn's worth at a time onto the global
//   run queue, the last of each batch carrying the rest, so a fan-in is not woken all at once to overload it again
//   (ponyc #2980). There is no operation that applies pressure by hand, whose cycles deadlocked ponyc (#3382).
//
// The bound, in messages: while a service holds turnBehaviours or more it cannot catch up, and every send to it is slowed,
// so it holds at most turnBehaviours plus what its senders add in such a stretch. Its length makes it behind, so it drains
// on, and is muted, letting its senders go (the progress argument), only once its queue is empty. A task adds the message
// it parks with, and one more each time such a mute lets it go; a service, the messages of its handlers from its first
// slowed send to its mute, which is that one handler or, if it drains on, that one and those it holds, whose own senders
// are slowed in turn, and one handler more each time such a mute lets it go. So main and a slow consumer: turnBehaviours
// + 1; main, a relay and the consumer: turnBehaviours + 2 at the relay and 2 * turnBehaviours + 4 at the consumer; a chain
// adds a stage's bound at each stage. A service is listed on one receiver at a time, so a second receiver that is behind
// gets that service's next handler after each unmute by the first, and its bound counts on how often the first catches
// up. A cycle whose handlers send more than they take grows, which no bound in messages could stop without stopping the
// program. A kind of a coalescing policy adds one message to each bound, its pending word's, which its sends queue without
// being slowed; a kind whose policy refuses or discards adds none.
//
// Why muting never stops the program, with no service, task or behaviour that can run (the progress argument): a sender
// is listed on a receiver under the receiver's lock while the receiver slows senders, and the receiver's catching up
// takes its whole list under that lock, so every entry is unmuted once its receiver catches up. A receiver that is not
// muted runs and catches up, since a handler never waits on another service. So a muted sender could wait for ever only
// in a cycle of muted services, each listed on the next for good. A service is muted under its own lock, which lets go
// every sender listed on it so far (muteService), so whoever stays listed on a muted service was listed after its mute,
// and was itself muted later still, when its handler ended. Around a cycle each mute would come after the next one's:
// none closes, whatever the rule that made the receivers behind. Tasks wait on no one but a receiver. A muted behaviour
// stays counted among the live ones, so the program does not end before it has run.

// The gate's flags, above every count of messages: serviceClosed once the service is shut down, serviceBehind while a
// turn ended at turnBehaviours with messages queued, serviceMuted while a message of the service waits, its release kept,
// for a service it sent to. serviceQueued masks the count below them.
let serviceClosed = 0x4000000000000000 as Count

let serviceBehind = 0x2000000000000000 as Count

let serviceMuted = 0x1000000000000000 as Count

let serviceQueued = 0x0fffffffffffffff as Count

// The words rt.service.reserve gives: a place taken, or the case of SendError plus 1 (sendServiceStopped of the
// built-ins).
let sendAccepted = 0 as Count

let sendStopped = 1 as Count

// noService is a green thread's service while it runs no message (greenServiceOffset).
let noService = 0 as Count

// A list of slowed senders (serviceMutedOffset) is a ring of entries: a service plus senderService, linked through its
// serviceLinkOffset, or a task's green thread, linked through its run queue link, which nothing reads while it is parked.
// mutedLock is added to the ring's word while one holds it; an address is a multiple of a word, so neither tag is part of
// one.
let noSender = 0 as Count

let senderService = 2 as Count

let mutedLock = 1 as Count

// What a service holds in its serviceWaitOffset besides the record of its kept message: nothing, or that it is listed on a
// service that slows it.
let waitNone = 0 as Count

let waitListed = 1 as Count

// behaviourRelease's answers to a behaviour's thunk: the cowns are handed on, or kept while the behaviour is muted.
let releaseHandedOn = 0 as Count

let releaseHeld = 1 as Count

// serviceCreate is a service of one owner with no message queued, holding state: the state the factory gave when it ran on
// the spawner, or 0 until the behaviour that runs the factory on the service stores it.
function serviceCreate(state Count) returns Count
	let service = alloc(serviceBytes)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownCountOffset, value: firstOwner)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownTailOffset, value: notQueued)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownEndingOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownValueOffset, value: state)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceMostOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitNone)
	return service
end 'serviceCreate'

// serviceReserve takes a place in the mailbox for a message, by a compare-exchange of the gate, unless the service is shut
// down. The gate is read once, as an acquire, and then by each compare-exchange that fails, so a send takes one locked
// operation on the gate when no other send races it. The word it took the place with slows the sender when it is
// turnBehaviours or more: a flag below serviceClosed, or that many messages queued before this one.
function serviceReserve(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1))
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			if gate >= turnBehaviours 'behind'
				noteQueued(handle, queued: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate and serviceQueued, right: 1))
				senderSlowed(handle)
			end 'behind'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'serviceReserve'

// Overload policies (README, "Overload policies"). A send of a kind whose type names a policy other than Keep takes its
// place by serviceTryReserve, which is serviceReserve while the service is not behind, so such a send costs what any other
// does then. While it is behind, the send takes no place and asks the policy's admit, which the compiler calls directly,
// and does what it answers: queue (serviceReserve, which slows the sender), refuse or discard (nothing is taken), or, for a
// kind of a CoalescingPolicy, replace the pending one (servicePlace, servicePutPending). A service whose type has such kinds
// holds a pending word for each after its own words (serviceCreatePending): the box of the arguments of the one message of
// the kind sent while it was behind that has not run, or 0. Each word that goes from 0 to a box has one behaviour queued for
// it, which takes the box (serviceTakePending), so a send that finds a box there replaces it and gives its place back. A
// send of the kind that finds a box there replaces it whether the service is behind or not (servicePendingHeld), since the
// box was sent before it and its behaviour is queued ahead: a sender's messages of the kind run in the order it sent them.
let sendBehind = 3 as Count

function servicePendingHeld(handle Count, slot Count) returns bool
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot)) != 0
end 'servicePendingHeld'

function serviceCreatePending(state Count, words Count) returns Count
	let service = alloc(pendingOffset(words))
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownCountOffset, value: firstOwner)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownTailOffset, value: notQueued)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownEndingOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: cownValueOffset, value: state)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceMostOffset, value: 0)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: noSender)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitNone)
	var i = 0 as Count
	while i < words 'words'
		__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: pendingOffset(i), value: 0)
		i = __Raw.wrappingAdd(i, right: 1)
	end 'words'
	return service
end 'serviceCreatePending'

function pendingOffset(slot Count) returns Count
	return try serviceBytes + slot * wordBytes otherwise panic("a pending word past memory")
end 'pendingOffset'

// serviceTryReserve is serviceReserve while the service is not behind; while it is, it takes no place and gives sendBehind.
function serviceTryReserve(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		if gate >= turnBehaviours 'behind'
			return sendBehind
		end 'behind'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1))
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'serviceTryReserve'

function serviceQueuedCount(handle Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset) and serviceQueued
end 'serviceQueuedCount'

function serviceTurn() returns Count
	return turnBehaviours
end 'serviceTurn'

// servicePlace takes a place for the message of a pending word, unless the service is shut down, and never slows the
// sender: the message replaces any other of its kind that waits.
function servicePlace(handle Count) returns Count
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while true 'gate'
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return sendStopped
		end 'closed'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: 1))
		if seen == gate 'taken'
			return sendAccepted
		end 'taken'
		gate = seen
	end 'gate'
	return sendAccepted
end 'servicePlace'

function serviceUnplace(handle Count)
	_ = __Raw.atomicAdd(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: stepDown)
end 'serviceUnplace'

function servicePutPending(handle Count, slot Count, box Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.atomicExchange(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: box)
end 'servicePutPending'

function serviceTakePending(state Count, slot Count) returns Count
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	let box = __Raw.atomicExchange(handle, offset: pendingOffset(slot), value: 0)
	if box == 0 'empty'
		panic("a pending message's behaviour found its word empty")
	end 'empty'
	return box
end 'serviceTakePending'

// serviceUnderLoad is whether the service whose message the green thread runs slows its senders, as a send to it would
// find; off a message it is false.
function serviceUnderLoad() returns bool
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let g = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextGreenOffset)
	if g == noGreen 'off'
		return false
	end 'off'
	let service = messageOf(g)
	if service == noService 'task'
		return false
	end 'task'
	let gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	return gate < serviceClosed and gate >= turnBehaviours
end 'serviceUnderLoad'

// noteQueued keeps the most messages a slowed send found queued with its own. Every count past turnBehaviours is reached
// by a slowed send, so the most is exact once it passes a turn's worth, and a send that is not slowed writes nothing.
function noteQueued(handle Count, queued Count)
	var most = __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceMostOffset)
	while queued > most 'raise'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceMostOffset, expected: most, desired: queued)
		if seen == most 'done'
			return
		end 'done'
		most = seen
	end 'raise'
end 'noteQueued'

function serviceMostQueued(handle Count) returns Count
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(handle, offset: serviceMostOffset)
end 'serviceMostQueued'

// serviceStart gives a message's place in the mailbox back as its behaviour starts, and names the service as the one the
// green thread runs a message of, and honours a stop the monitor asked for (honourAsk). state is the address of the
// cown's value, which the behaviour is given.
function serviceStart(state Count)
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	_ = __Raw.atomicAdd(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: stepDown)
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let g = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextGreenOffset)
	__Raw.storeWord(g, offset: greenServiceOffset, value: handle)
	honourAsk(context, g: g)
end 'serviceStart'

// serviceNew, serviceTaken and cownRelease are the entries that the code of the seed's compiler calls (seed/README.md),
// whose thunks cannot keep a release: a mailbox's capacity is ignored, and a message names no service, so none of that code
// is muted or makes a service behind.
function serviceNew(state Count, capacity Count) returns Count
	return serviceCreate(state)
end 'serviceNew'

function serviceTaken(state Count)
	let handle = __Raw.wrappingSub(state, right: cownValueOffset)
	_ = __Raw.atomicAdd(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: stepDown)
end 'serviceTaken'

function cownRelease(record Count)
	releaseCowns(record, service: noService)
end 'cownRelease'

// serviceShutdown closes the mailbox once: the bit stays set, and the messages queued before it still run.
function serviceShutdown(handle Count)
	while true 'gate'
		let gate = __Raw.atomicAdd(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, value: 0)
		if gate >= serviceClosed 'closed'
			return
		end 'closed'
		if __Raw.compareExchange(handle, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: serviceClosed)) == gate 'done'
			return
		end 'done'
	end 'gate'
end 'serviceShutdown'

// senderSlowed is what a send to a service that is behind does to its sender, once its message has its place: another
// service whose message runs is listed on it, to be muted at the end of a handler, and a task parks until the receiver
// catches up.
function senderSlowed(receiver Count)
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let g = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextGreenOffset)
	if g == noGreen 'off'
		return
	end 'off'
	let sender = messageOf(g)
	if sender != noService 'message'
		listService(receiver, sender: sender)
		return
	end 'message'
	parkSender(context, receiver: receiver, g: g)
end 'senderSlowed'

// messageOf is the service whose message g runs, or noService for a task: a green thread that runs no behaviour, or one that
// is no message of the service it last started one of.
function messageOf(g Count) returns Count
	if __Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenKindOffset) != greenBehaviour 'task'
		return noService
	end 'task'
	let service = __Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenServiceOffset)
	if service == noService or not isMessageOf(__Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenArgOffset), service: service) 'other'
		return noService
	end 'other'
	return service
end 'messageOf'

function isMessageOf(record Count, service Count) returns bool
	return __Raw.loadWord(record, offset: behaviourCownCountOffset) == 1 and __Raw.loadWord(record, offset: cownOffset(1, index: 0)) == service
end 'isMessageOf'

// listService lists a sender on a receiver that is behind, once, however many such receivers it sends to: it stays listed
// until the receiver catches up, and is muted at the end of the first of its handlers that leaves it not exempt
// (behaviourRelease). A service never waits on itself.
function listService(receiver Count, sender Count)
	if sender == receiver or __Raw.loadAcquire(sender, offset: serviceWaitOffset) != waitNone 'listed'
		return
	end 'listed'
	let newest = lockMuted(receiver)
	if not slowsSenders(receiver) 'caughtUp'
		unlockMuted(receiver, newest: newest)
		return
	end 'caughtUp'
	__Raw.storeWord(sender, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitListed)
	unlockMuted(receiver, newest: ringAdd(newest, entry: __Raw.wrappingAdd(sender, right: senderService)))
end 'listService'

// parkSender parks a task on a receiver that is behind until it catches up. The unmute that readies it may hand it the rest
// of the list, which it unmutes in turn.
function parkSender(context Count, receiver Count, g Count)
	__Raw.storeWord(g, offset: greenMuteOffset, value: noSender)
	let newest = lockMuted(receiver)
	if not slowsSenders(receiver) 'caughtUp'
		unlockMuted(receiver, newest: newest)
		return
	end 'caughtUp'
	unlockMuted(receiver, newest: ringAdd(newest, entry: g))
	park(context)
	let rest = __Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenMuteOffset)
	if rest != noSender 'rest'
		__Raw.storeWord(g, offset: greenMuteOffset, value: noSender)
		unmuteFrom(rest)
	end 'rest'
end 'parkSender'

// slowsSenders is whether a service is behind or muted, read under the lock of its list, which its catching up and its
// mute take the list under.
function slowsSenders(service Count) returns bool
	let gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	return (gate and (serviceBehind or serviceMuted)) != 0 or (gate and serviceQueued) >= turnBehaviours
end 'slowsSenders'

// drainsOn is whether a listed service runs on at the end of its message's handler instead of being muted: messages are
// queued after the message, and it is behind itself, by its turns or the length of its queue, or has senders waiting on
// it. Its senders are slowed, so it passes on only what it holds, and it is muted once that is gone. The queue is read and
// not the gate's count, which holds the message of a task parked at its send before it is queued.
function drainsOn(service Count, record Count) returns bool
	if queueEmpty(record) 'drained'
		return false
	end 'drained'
	let gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	return (gate and serviceBehind) != 0 or (gate and serviceQueued) >= turnBehaviours or __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset) > mutedLock
end 'drainsOn'

// queueEmpty is whether no message is queued, or being queued, after a behaviour of one cown.
function queueEmpty(record Count) returns bool
	let slot = requestSlot(record, index: 0)
	return __Raw.loadAcquire(slot, offset: requestNextOffset) == notQueued and __Raw.loadAcquire(__Raw.loadWord(slot, offset: requestCownOffset), offset: cownTailOffset) == slot
end 'queueEmpty'

// behaviourRelease is what a behaviour's thunk calls after its body. A message of a service listed on one that is behind is
// muted here unless the service drains on, or the receiver caught up while it ran: its cowns, holds and record are kept,
// the thunk's finish runs with the record once it is unmuted, and the answer is releaseHeld. Any other behaviour hands its
// cowns on (releaseCowns).
function behaviourRelease(record Count, finish Count) returns Count
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	let g = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextGreenOffset)
	if g == noGreen 'off'
		releaseCowns(record, service: noService)
		return releaseHandedOn
	end 'off'
	let service = __Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenServiceOffset)
	if service != noService and __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset) == waitListed and isMessageOf(record, service: service) and not drainsOn(service, record: record) 'listed'
		if keepsRelease(context, record: record, service: service, finish: finish) 'kept'
			return releaseHeld
		end 'kept'
	end 'listed'
	releaseCowns(record, service: service)
	return releaseHandedOn
end 'behaviourRelease'

// keepsRelease mutes a listed service as its message's handler ends, and says whether it did: not when the service it
// waits on caught up first. The muted behaviour stays counted among the live ones until its finish has run. The muted flag
// is set before the record is offered, so the unmute that takes the record clears it after.
function keepsRelease(context Count, record Count, service Count, finish Count) returns bool
	__Raw.storeWord(record, offset: behaviourRunOffset, value: finish)
	stepLive(context, change: 1)
	muteService(service)
	if __Raw.compareExchange(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset, expected: waitListed, desired: record) == waitListed 'kept'
		return true
	end 'kept'
	clearGateFlag(service, flag: serviceMuted)
	stepLive(context, change: stepDown)
	return false
end 'keepsRelease'

// muteService marks a service muted and lets go the senders listed on it, under its lock, so that whoever is listed on it
// from then on was listed after its mute (the progress argument).
function muteService(service Count)
	let newest = lockMuted(service)
	setGateFlag(service, flag: serviceMuted)
	unlockMuted(service, newest: noSender)
	unmuteRing(newest)
end 'muteService'

// behaviourResumed is what the finish of a behaviour's thunk calls once its service is unmuted: its cowns are handed on, its
// release counting as its service's own messages' do, so one that empties the queue makes the service catch up, which a
// task parked on the service while it was muted waits for; and the rest of the list its unmute was handed is unmuted.
function behaviourResumed(record Count)
	let service = __Raw.loadWord(record, offset: cownOffset(1, index: 0))
	let rest = __Raw.loadWord(service, offset: serviceLinkOffset)
	__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: noSender)
	releaseCowns(record, service: service)
	if rest != noSender 'rest'
		unmuteFrom(rest)
	end 'rest'
end 'behaviourResumed'

// markBehind makes the service whose message a turn ran last behind, when the behaviour the turn hands on is its next
// message: the turn ended at turnBehaviours with messages still queued.
function markBehind(g Count, record Count)
	let service = __Raw.loadWord(g, offset: greenServiceOffset)
	if service != noService and isMessageOf(record, service: service) 'next'
		setGateFlag(service, flag: serviceBehind)
	end 'next'
end 'markBehind'

// caughtUpIfSlowing is what the release that empties a service's queue does: the service is no longer behind, and its
// senders are unmuted.
function caughtUpIfSlowing(service Count)
	if (__Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset) and serviceBehind) != 0 or __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset) != noSender 'slowing'
		let newest = lockMuted(service)
		clearGateFlag(service, flag: serviceBehind)
		unlockMuted(service, newest: noSender)
		unmuteRing(newest)
	end 'slowing'
end 'caughtUpIfSlowing'

// unmuteRing unmutes the entries of a ring taken off its service, oldest first.
function unmuteRing(newest Count)
	if newest != noSender 'senders'
		let oldest = senderLink(newest)
		setSenderLink(newest, link: noSender)
		unmuteFrom(oldest)
	end 'senders'
end 'unmuteRing'

// unmuteFrom unmutes the entries of a list in order: a muted service's kept message and a parked task go to the global run
// queue, and a service still running is no longer listed. After a turn's worth, the last carries the rest, which it
// unmutes once it runs.
function unmuteFrom(first Count)
	let context = __Raw.threadContext()
	var entry = first
	var woken = 0 as Count
	while entry != noSender 'entries'
		let next = senderLink(entry)
		var queued = true
		if (entry and senderService) != 0 'service'
			let service = __Raw.wrappingSub(entry, right: senderService)
			let kept = __Raw.compareExchange(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset, expected: waitListed, desired: waitNone)
			if kept == waitListed 'running'
				queued = false
			end 'running' else 'muted'
				__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceWaitOffset, value: waitNone)
				clearGateFlag(service, flag: serviceMuted)
				woken = __Raw.wrappingAdd(woken, right: 1)
				__Raw.storeWord(service, offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: next if woken == turnBehaviours else noSender)
				onGlobalQueue(context, entry: behaviourEntry(kept))
			end 'muted'
		end 'service' else 'task'
			woken = __Raw.wrappingAdd(woken, right: 1)
			__Raw.storeWord(entry, offset: greenMuteOffset, value: next if woken == turnBehaviours else noSender)
			if markReady(entry) 'parked'
				onGlobalQueue(context, entry: entry)
			end 'parked'
		end 'task'
		if queued and woken == turnBehaviours 'batch'
			return
		end 'batch'
		entry = next
	end 'entries'
end 'unmuteFrom'

// onGlobalQueue puts a run queue's entry at the tail of the global run queue, which is FIFO, and wakes a thread for it. A
// kept behaviour is counted among the live ones already.
function onGlobalQueue(context Count, entry Count)
	let scheduler = __Raw.loadWord(context, offset: contextSchedulerOffset)
	lockScheduler(scheduler)
	globalPut(scheduler, g: entry)
	unlockScheduler(scheduler)
	wakeProc(scheduler)
end 'onGlobalQueue'

// lockMuted takes the lock of a service's list of slowed senders and gives the ring's newest entry; only its holder
// changes the ring, and unlockMuted lets it go with the newest entry it leaves.
function lockMuted(service Count) returns Count
	while true 'lock'
		let word = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset)
		if (word and mutedLock) == 0 and __Raw.compareExchange(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset, expected: word, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(word, right: mutedLock)) == word 'taken'
			return word
		end 'taken'
		__Raw.spinPause()
	end 'lock'
	return noSender
end 'lockMuted'

function unlockMuted(service Count, newest Count)
	__Raw.storeRelease(service, offset: serviceMutedOffset, value: newest)
end 'unlockMuted'

// ringAdd adds an entry after the newest of a ring, as the newest, and gives it.
function ringAdd(newest Count, entry Count) returns Count
	if newest == noSender 'first'
		setSenderLink(entry, link: entry)
		return entry
	end 'first'
	setSenderLink(entry, link: senderLink(newest))
	setSenderLink(newest, link: entry)
	return entry
end 'ringAdd'

function senderLink(entry Count) returns Count
	if (entry and senderService) != 0 'service'
		return __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.wrappingSub(entry, right: senderService), offset: serviceLinkOffset)
	end 'service'
	return __Raw.loadWord(entry, offset: greenLinkOffset)
end 'senderLink'

function setSenderLink(entry Count, link Count)
	if (entry and senderService) != 0 'service'
		__Raw.storeWord(__Raw.wrappingSub(entry, right: senderService), offset: serviceLinkOffset, value: link)
		return
	end 'service'
	__Raw.storeWord(entry, offset: greenLinkOffset, value: link)
end 'setSenderLink'

// setGateFlag and clearGateFlag set and clear a flag of a service's gate, beside the sends that step its count.
function setGateFlag(service Count, flag Count)
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while (gate and flag) == 0 'set'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(service, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingAdd(gate, right: flag))
		if seen == gate 'done'
			return
		end 'done'
		gate = seen
	end 'set'
end 'setGateFlag'

function clearGateFlag(service Count, flag Count)
	var gate = __Raw.loadAcquire(service, offset: serviceGateOffset)
	while (gate and flag) != 0 'clear'
		let seen = __Raw.compareExchange(service, offset: serviceGateOffset, expected: gate, desired: __Raw.wrappingSub(gate, right: flag))
		if seen == gate 'done'
			return
		end 'done'
		gate = seen
	end 'clear'
end 'clearGateFlag'

// replyNew is the cell of a reply: a green thread's record that is awaited and never runs, so it holds no stack and does
// not count among the live green threads; the behaviour of the message is the one that counts.
function replyNew() returns Count
	return newGreen(__Raw.threadContext(), code: 0, kind: greenAwaited)
end 'replyNew'

// replyGive finishes a reply as greenMain and finished finish a coroutine: the value and the error first, then the
// exchange of the waiter, so the awaiter either is readied here or finds the cell finished.
function replyGive(promise Count, value Count, error Count)
	__Raw.storeWord(promise, offset: greenResultOffset, value: value)
	__Raw.storeWord(promise, offset: greenErrorOffset, value: error)
	wakeWaiter(__Raw.threadContext(), waiter: __Raw.atomicExchange(promise, offset: greenWaiterOffset, value: greenFinished))
end 'replyGive'
